    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7e6e3ed8_47add3ef",
        "filename": "src/sksl/sksl_graphite_frag.sksl",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 125,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-10T18:08:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we need to use a half3 to control these behaviors.\n\n - For the case where we don\u0027t have a gamut+TF in the middle, we should never be seeing doUnpremul and doPremul at the same time.\n - If we are forcing the alpha to 1, neither unpremul or premul are going to have an effect so they could be skipped.\n \nPossible actual values for the uniform are then\n    {0,0,0} // identity\n    {1,0,0} // make opaque\n    {0,1,0} // unpremul\n    {0,0,1} // premul\nif we copy this structure to the universal shader, then `{0,1,1}` also becomes a possible value.\n\nI think we could encode all of this with a half2 `{opaqueOrUnpremul, doPremul}`.\n - If it\u0027s supposed to be opaque, the first value is still 1.\n - If it\u0027s supposed to be unpremul, the first value is \u003c 0.\n - If neither opaque or unpremul, then the first value is 0.\n - The second value is 0 or 1, just like you have it.\n \nOther than changing the if condition for doUnpremul to \u003c, I think everything else works out with the opaque and premul cases.\n\nI couldn\u0027t think of a way that handled every case with just a single value. It wasn\u0027t able to differentiate the {0,0,1} vs {0,0,0} case and wouldn\u0027t have applied to the universal function\u0027s {0,1,1} requirement either.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 125,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "68dd6943dfbe6d3304ccc35f113ae815f00d8967",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
